{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775324575-ChvrG9lHvDxJ7ymxBcZl","role":"assistant","created":1775324590,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the data I need. Let me extract the convertible foreign currencies values for CY 1963-1977 from both documents.\n\nFrom the 1970 bulletin (page 94), I can see data for 1963-1969:\n- 1963: 212\n- 1964: 432\n- 1965: 781\n- 1966: 1,321\n- 1967: 2,345\n- 1968: 3,528\n- 1969: 2,781 (or 2,781 7/ which means 2,781 with a footnote)\n\nFrom the 1978 bulletin (page 91), I can see data for 1968-1977:\n- 1968: 3,528\n- 1969: 2,781 7/.\n- 1970: 629\n- 1971: 276 8/\n- 1972: 241\n- 1973: 8\n- 1974: 5\n- 1975: 80\n- 1976: 320\n- 1977: 18\n\nSo for CY 1963-1977, I need:\n- 1963: 212\n- 1964: 432\n- 1965: 781\n- 1966: 1,321\n- 1967: 2,345\n- 1968: 3,528\n- 1969: 2,781\n- 1970: 629\n- 1971: 276\n- 1972: 241\n- 1973: 8\n- 1974: 5\n- 1975: 80\n- 1976: 320\n- 1977: 18\n\nThat's 15 data points (1963-1977 inclusive).\n\n\nNow I need to calculate the Fisher-Pearson adjusted skewness coefficient using sample standard deviation. The formula requires computing the sample standard deviation, then finding the sum of cubed deviations from the mean divided by s^3, and finally applying the adjustment factor n/(n-1)(n-2).\n\nWith n=15, I'll need the mean of my data set: 212, 432, 781, 1321, 2345, 3528, 2781, 629, 276, 241, 8, 5, 80, 320, 18. First, I'll sum these values to find the mean, then calculate each deviation from that mean, cube those deviations, and sum them.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_e7b0bb285bed4e4c82dab65b","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport json\n\n# Data from both bulletins for convertible foreign currencies (in millions)\n# 1963-1977 from the tables\ndata = {\n    1963: 212,\n    1964: 432,\n    1965: 781,\n    1966: 1321,\n    1967: 2345,\n    1968: 3528,\n    1969: 2781,\n    1970: 629,\n    1971: 276,\n    1972: 241,\n    1973: 8,\n    1974: 5,\n    1975: 80,\n    1976: 320,\n    1977: 18\n}\n\nvalues = list(data.values())\nprint(\"Convertible foreign currencies (in millions) for CY 1963-1977:\")\nfor year, val in data.items():\n    print(f\"  {year}: {val}\")\n\nn = len(values)\nprint(f\"\\nn = {n}\")\n\n# Calculate mean\nmean = sum(values) / n\nprint(f\"Mean = {mean}\")\n\n# Calculate sample standard deviation\n# First, sum of squared deviations\nsum_sq_dev = sum((x - mean)**2 for x in values)\nsample_std = (sum_sq_dev / (n - 1)) ** 0.5\nprint(f\"Sample standard deviation = {sample_std}\")\n\n# Fisher-Pearson adjusted skewness formula:\n# G1 = (n / ((n-1)(n-2))) * sum((xi - xbar)^3) / s^3\n\n# Calculate sum of cubed deviations\nsum_cubed_dev = sum((x - mean)**3 for x in values)\nprint(f\"Sum of cubed deviations = {sum_cubed_dev}\")\n\n# Calculate skewness\nnumerator = n * sum_cubed_dev\ndenominator = (n - 1) * (n - 2) * (sample_std ** 3)\n\nG1 = numerator / denominator\n\nprint(f\"\\nFisher-Pearson adjusted skewness = {G1}\")\nprint(f\"Rounded to 3 decimal places = {round(G1, 3)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
